# Smoke tests = browser automation only

When verifying that a **shipped UI surface or operator workflow** works end-to-end
(smoke / soak / “prove it in the running system”):

## Required

- Use **browser automation** (Playwright MCP / cursor-ide-browser) against the
  live web app (`http://localhost:3000` or the running topology).
- Assert what the **operator sees**: labels, tiles, tables, empty states,
  error banners, testids, navigation — not JSON payloads.

## Prohibited as smoke proof

- `curl` / `Invoke-RestMethod` / `fetch` against API routes
- One-shot Python/Node scripts that call services or `AsyncSession` directly
- Celery sync / in-process orchestrator runs treated as UI smoke
- Claiming “smoke passed” from pytest or API response shape alone

## Still allowed (not smoke)

| Kind | Purpose |
|------|---------|
| **Unit / focused pytest** | Logic, formulas, mocked read models |
| **SQL path validation** | Real DB execution for VALUES/CASE/type correctness (project SQL rule) — proves SQL, **not** UI smoke |
| **API contract tests** | Schema / status codes in the test suite |

If the web or API process is down, **start the app stack and open the page in the
browser** — do not substitute a backend call for the missing UI check.

## Done bar

A UI/feature unit is not smoke-verified until a browser session has loaded the
owning route and confirmed the visible outcome that the unit claimed to ship.
